<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Custom theme attributes for the pastel stat pills.

    **Why these exist.** Material 3 ships a fixed set of colour *roles* — primary, secondary,
    tertiary, error and the surfaces. Those answer the question "how important is this?", and the
    app already uses them for exactly that. A stat pill asks a different question: a row showing
    age, weight and species needs three colours that are *distinguishable from one another*, all
    equally quiet, none of them implying one fact matters more than the next. There is no Material
    role for "one of several equal categories", so the app declares its own.

    **Why attributes rather than colours.** A layout could name @color/pill_light_mint directly, but
    that colour is only correct in light mode — the pill would keep its pale wash on a dark
    background and the text on it would vanish. Declaring an *attribute* here and giving it a value
    in both values/themes.xml and values-night/themes.xml means a layout writes
    ?attr/colorPillMint once and gets the right value in either theme, which is the same mechanism
    the Material roles themselves use.

    Each pill has a pair: the background, and the text colour guaranteed legible on it. They are
    always used together — text on colorPillMint must be colorOnPillMint and nothing else.
-->
<resources>

    <!--
        The accent as a *foreground* colour, for text and icons that should read as accented.

        Declared for the same reason as the pills: Material has no role for it. colorPrimary is the
        accent, but this app's accent is a bright yellow chosen to be filled behind dark text — and
        it is far too light to *be* text on a white card. colorOnPrimary is the wrong answer too,
        since that colour is guaranteed legible on the yellow, not on the surface.

        So the app declares its own, a deeper amber of the same hue. See colors.xml for the values
        and the contrast reasoning.
    -->
    <attr name="colorAccentText" format="color" />

    <!--
        The "this one is chosen" fill, used on the Tasks tab for the selected day in the week strip,
        the selected filter chips, and the Delegate button.

        Declared because no Material role means "selected, strongly". In the light theme it is the
        app's near-black with the yellow accent written on it; in the dark theme a near-black fill
        would vanish into the near-black page, so the pair flips to a yellow fill with dark text. Both
        are the highest-contrast thing on their page, which is the property that actually matters.

        colorOnSelectedContainer is the accented text on that fill (yellow on black / dark on yellow).
        colorOnSelectedContainerNeutral is plain text on the same fill (white on black / dark on
        yellow), for a button label that should read as a word rather than as a highlight.
    -->
    <attr name="colorSelectedContainer" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnSelectedContainer" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnSelectedContainerNeutral" format="color" />

    <!--
        The *unavailable* version of that same strong fill, used by the sign-in screens' main button
        while it cannot be pressed yet ("VERIFY CODE" before all six digits are in).

        Declared because Material's own disabled treatment — the enabled colour at low opacity — turns
        a near-black button into a washed-out grey smear on the cream page, which reads as broken
        rather than as "not yet". The design instead shows a solid charcoal with pale text: clearly the
        same button, clearly resting. In dark mode the enabled fill is yellow, so the resting fill is a
        muted charcoal a step above the page rather than a dim yellow.
    -->
    <attr name="colorDisabledContainer" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnDisabledContainer" format="color" />

    <!-- Mint — the app uses this one for a pet's age. -->
    <attr name="colorPillMint" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillMint" format="color" />

    <!-- Sky — weight. -->
    <attr name="colorPillSky" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillSky" format="color" />

    <!-- Lilac — species. -->
    <attr name="colorPillLilac" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillLilac" format="color" />

    <!-- Peach — outstanding care. -->
    <attr name="colorPillPeach" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillPeach" format="color" />

    <!-- Pale green — the profile screen's "VERIFIED" pill. Not part of the four-hue stat-pill family
         above, which exists so three facts side by side can be told apart; this one always means the
         same thing, and green is what it means. It is exposed the same way for the same reason: the
         light and dark values are not a lighter and darker version of each other, so a layout has to
         reach them through a role rather than naming a colour. -->
    <attr name="colorPillVerified" format="color" />
    <attr name="colorOnPillVerified" format="color" />

</resources>
